==== S617 equinumerosity / equidecomposability bridge ====
Equinumerosity audit: three noninterchangeable quotients
  T_burnside is the A000568 tournament count from odd-order cycle types.
  degree-even means Euler/cycle-space graph classes, not Royle-even graphs.
  naive-even is the even-order Burnside complement; it is also not Royle-even.

   n     graphs   T_burnside   T_enum  degree-even   naive-even
   3          4            2        2            2            2
   4         11            4        4            3            7
   5         34           12       12            7           22
   6        156           56       56           16          100
   7       1044          456        -            -          588
   8      12346         6880        -            -         5466
   9     274668       191536        -            -        83132
  10   12005168      9733056        -            -      2272112
  11 1018997864    903753248        -            -    115244616
  12 165091172592 154108311168        -            -  10982861424

  Findings:
    degree-even classes agree with tournaments only at n=3 in this range.
    naive-even equals graphs - tournaments, not tournaments, once n>=4.
    Therefore equinumerosity needs the Royle-even intrinsic graph property,
    not the cycle-space projection and not the Burnside parity split.

Equidecomposability audit: retained tournament invariants
  H is the volume shadow. beta1 is the first Dehn/scissors obstruction.
  c3-poly is the disjoint directed-3-cycle packet polynomial 1+c1*x+c2*x^2.

   n   iso     H   (H,b1)    (H,b1,c3) split H-values by beta1
   3     2     2        2            2 {}
   4     4     3        3            3 {}
   5    12     7        8            9 {9: [0, 1]}
   6    56    19       24           29 {17: [0, 1], 23: [0, 1], 33: [0, 1], 37: [0, 1], 45: [0, 1]}

  n=5 split ledger
    H=9: beta1 iso-counts={0: 1, 1: 1}, labeled-mass={0: 120, 1: 120}

  n=6 split ledger
    H=17: beta1 iso-counts={0: 1, 1: 1}, labeled-mass={0: 720, 1: 720}
    H=23: beta1 iso-counts={0: 2, 1: 2}, labeled-mass={0: 1440, 1: 1440}
    H=33: beta1 iso-counts={0: 3, 1: 2}, labeled-mass={0: 2160, 1: 480}
    H=37: beta1 iso-counts={0: 4, 1: 1}, labeled-mass={0: 2880, 1: 720}
    H=45: beta1 iso-counts={0: 1, 1: 1}, labeled-mass={0: 240, 1: 240}

  Findings:
    At n=5, H=9 already splits into beta1=0 and beta1=1 scissors classes.
    At n=6, five H-values split by beta1: 17, 23, 33, 37, 45.
    Adding the c3 packet polynomial refines 24 (H,beta1) classes to 29.
    Thus equal H is equinumerosity-like volume data, not equidecomposition.

Tournament Analysis on quotient lenses
  Vertices are quotient choices, not runners, arcs, or graph vertices.
  Pairwise observable: majority over cardinal evidence, constructivity,
  H preservation, scissors refinement, cross-domain reach, and risk.
  score=6 lens=Full tournament isomorphism; features=(card=5, map=5, H=5, scissors=5, cross=2, lowrisk=5)
  score=5 lens=(H,beta1) scissors quotient; features=(card=4, map=4, H=5, scissors=5, cross=5, lowrisk=4)
  score=4 lens=H-volume quotient; features=(card=4, map=5, H=5, scissors=1, cross=4, lowrisk=4)
  score=3 lens=(H,beta1,c3) packet quotient; features=(card=4, map=4, H=5, scissors=5, cross=4, lowrisk=3)
  score=2 lens=Degree-even cycle projection; features=(card=2, map=5, H=2, scissors=2, cross=4, lowrisk=3)
  score=1 lens=Royle-even cardinal theorem; features=(card=5, map=1, H=1, scissors=1, cross=5, lowrisk=2)
  score=0 lens=Naive Burnside parity split; features=(card=1, map=4, H=0, scissors=0, cross=2, lowrisk=1)
  score histogram: {0: 1, 1: 1, 2: 1, 3: 1, 4: 1, 5: 1, 6: 1}
  directed 3-cycles: 0
  SCC sizes: [1, 1, 1, 1, 1, 1, 1]
  Hamiltonian path count: 1
  edge flips when risk is removed: 1
  Reading: full isomorphism is safest but too fine; (H,beta1) is the
    best working scissors quotient; Royle-even is cardinal signal only
    until a constructive predicate-preserving map is supplied.

Assumption challenge
  Do not make tournament vertices be runners or arcs by reflex.
  Alternative vertex sets considered:
    Burnside cycle types, tournament iso classes, degree-even graph classes,
    Royle-even graph classes, H-fibers, beta1 chains, disjoint c3 packets,
    OCF proof obligations, and quotient lenses.
  Preserved predicate:
    Equinumerosity preserves only a count unless the map also preserves H,
    beta1, packet data, or the target scissors class.
  Destroyed information:
    Degree-even projection forgets cut/score data; H forgets beta1;
    (H,beta1) still forgets some c3 packet structure at n=6.
  Challenged assumption:
    A000568-sized quotients are not automatically equivalent proof objects.

Hypothesis HYP-2187:
  Equinumerosity is a cardinal shadow; equidecomposability is a retained
  invariant quotient.  The bridge must be a fiber functor that records which
  side channels survive: score/cut data, beta1, and odd-cycle packets.
